---
import CodeStepper from "../../components/CodeStepper.astro";
import LessonLayout from "../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro";
import { pageUrl } from "../../lib/links";
import { algorithms } from "../../lessons/stacks/lesson";
---

<LessonLayout
  title="Stacks | A Level Computer Science"
  description="Learn how a stack works in Python, one line at a time, for Cambridge International A Level Computer Science (9618)."
>
  <main class="page">
    <p class="crumb"><a href={pageUrl()}>Cambridge A Level Computer Science (9618)</a> / Abstract data types</p>
    <h1 class="lesson-title">Stacks</h1>
    <div class="prose lesson-intro">
      <p class="lesson-lead">The last item added to a stack is the first one removed.</p>
      <p>
        Think of a pile of plates: you add a plate to the top, and you take a plate from the top.
        This rule is called last in, first out (LIFO). <strong>Push</strong> adds an item and <strong>Pop</strong> removes one. <strong>Peek</strong> looks at the top item without removing it, and <strong>IsEmpty</strong> and <strong>IsFull</strong> answer <code>True</code> or <code>False</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        In Python, you can build a stack from a fixed-size array and a pointer called <code>TopPointer</code>. The pointer can start at -1 and hold the index of the top item, or start at 0 and hold the index of the next free space.
        Exam papers may give the pointer another name, such as <code>TopOfStack</code>, so check where it starts and what it points at.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="lesson-section">
      <h2>Watch the code run</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        Select <strong>Next line</strong> to run the code one line at a time. The yellow line has
        just run, and the pink arrow shows where <code>TopPointer</code> is pointing.
      </p>
      <CodeStepper algorithms={algorithms} visual="stack" variables={["StackSize", "Item", "Removed"]} />
    </section>
  </main>
</LessonLayout>

